1 ASSIGN

   Creates a logical name, as DEFINE does, with its parameters the
   other way round.

   Format

     ASSIGN equivalence-string[,...] logical-name[:]

2 Parameters

 equivalence-string[,...]

   What the logical name stands for.  More than one makes the name a
   search list.  Unquoted text is made uppercase.

 logical-name[:]

   The name to define.  One trailing colon is removed, so ASSIGN DUA1:
   WORK: defines WORK.

2 Qualifiers
/EXECUTIVE_MODE

   Defines the name at executive mode.

/GROUP

   Defines the name in the group table, LNM$GROUP.

/JOB

   Defines the name in the job table, LNM$JOB, shared by the console's
   process and every subprocess it creates.

/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   Reports when the name replaces one defined before in the same table
   at the same mode (the default, /LOG).

/PROCESS

   Defines the name in the process table, LNM$PROCESS (the default).

/SUPERVISOR_MODE

   Defines the name at supervisor mode (the default).

/SYSTEM

   Defines the name in the system table, LNM$SYSTEM.

/TABLE

      /TABLE=name

   Defines the name in the table named, or the table a logical name
   such as LNM$PROCESS translates to.

/TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES

      /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(keyword[,...])

   CONCEALED shows the logical name in place of the device it stands
   for, and TERMINAL stops iterative translation at this name.  The
   attributes apply to every equivalence string.

   An equivalence string whose directory ends in a period (DUA1:[WORK.])
   makes a rooted name: the directory looks like a volume's master file
   directory, its subdirectories like top-level directories, the root
   itself is [000000], and nothing above it can be reached.  Such a name
   is usually concealed.

/USER_MODE

   Defines the name at user mode.  A user-mode name in the process
   table is deleted when the next image exits.

2 Examples

   1.$ ASSIGN DUA1:[WORK] WORK:

     Defines WORK as DUA1:[WORK] in the process table.

   2.$ ASSIGN/JOB DUA1:[DATA],DUA2:[DATA] DATA

     Defines DATA, a search list of two directories, in the job table.
